# Supplementary methods: Kinnex array integrity

These methods follow section 7 of the Supplementary Note ("Kinnex array integrity in FLIGHT-seq libraries"), where each analysis sits next to its figures and tables. The code is in this repository: `src/kinnex_qc` (adapter and primer search, chain classes, S-read checks), `study/pipeline` (stages 01 to 07 and their validation gates), `study/analyses`, `study/argentag`, `study/calibration` and `study/report`. `study/run_study.sh` runs them in order.

## Libraries and reads

FLIGHT-seq full-length cDNA from one BD Rhapsody cartridge was split after lysis into three technical replicates and concatenated with the Kinnex 8-fold kit with no TSO-TSO depletion (ND), a home-made biotin-Read1 pulldown (HM) or the Kinnex TSO artifact removal (kin); each library was sequenced on one Revio SMRT Cell. HiFi reads, fail reads, skera S-reads (skera 26.1.0, as delivered) and ZMW metrics were extracted from the instrument archives as plain files (`study/prepare_inputs.sh`). A ZMW was sampled when the splitmix64 hash of its number, XORed with a fixed salt, fell in the lowest {fraction|pct0} of the 64-bit range ({hifi_analysed_min|mio2} to {hifi_analysed_max|mio2} million HiFi reads per library). Every input file (HiFi reads, skera S-reads, non-passing reads) was read to the end and filtered by this rule, and lima and refine outputs were restricted to the same ZMWs. The share of the cell's HiFi reads in the sample, the sampling probability behind the w(T) weights, is the number of sampled HiFi reads over skera's whole-cell count. Fail reads were indexed in full and annotated on a separate 1-in-10 hash subsample of ZMWs.

## Calibration set

Comparisons that need the same reads under two methods (skera 1.2.0 against 26.1.0, skera's cuts against ours, molecules skera did not write through lima and refine) use the first {cal_hifi_reads|fmt} ND HiFi reads, with base-modification tags removed because skera 1.2.0 fails on them. These reads were annotated with stage 02 of the pipeline and matched by ZMW to the S-reads skera 26.1.0 delivered and to skera's verdict (`study/calibration/build_calibration_set.py`). They are not a random sample of the cell and serve only for comparisons within the same reads. At each skera cut with no adapter of ours within 5 bp, the gap between the two S-reads plus 5 bp on each side was aligned to every Kinnex adapter on both strands (edlib, infix mode) and the smallest edit distance kept (`study/analyses/false_cuts_calibration.py`).

## Segmentation

Every HiFi read was searched for the nine Kinnex adapters (A to H, Q; up to 2 edits), the TSO core `AAGCAGTGGTATCAACGCAGAGTAC` (up to 3 edits) and the Read1 primer `ACACGACGCTCTTCCGATCT` (up to 3 edits) on both strands with edlib. An adapter hit was kept only within 4 bp of a TSO or Read1 hit. Reads were cut at adapters into molecules, and each molecule was classed by the primers in its first and last 150 bp: proper (TSO at one end, Read1 at the other, opposite strands), TSO-TSO, R1-R1, TSO only, R1 only, or none; a TSO or Read1 inside a molecule marks it as fused. The bead cell label linkers and an anchored polyT were scored after Read1. For fail reads the TSO threshold was relaxed to 4 edits, chosen on synthetic arrays at 5 to 12% error (adapter and Read1 thresholds stayed, because relaxing them dropped precision below 0.99). Adapter chains were classed as complete, truncated, mirror, skipped slot, repeated or unresolved; a mirror is a pair of adjacent adapters with the same letter in opposite orientation. The classes were benchmarked on synthetic reads of known structure built from clean S-reads (`study/analyses/chain_benchmark.py`; Supplementary Table 4), and a random sample of 30 ND reads where skera's cuts and ours disagree was rendered for inspection (`study/analyses/discordant_reads.py`).

## skera's cuts and what they cost

skera's S-reads were laid over our molecules by their coordinates in the parent read (the documented `qs` and `qe` tags, https://skera.how/read-segments.html). A false cut is a gap between consecutive S-reads with no adapter of ours within 5 bp. For every false cut in complete arrays that skera passes, the molecule that holds it, the adapter of ours left inside the neighbouring S-read (the skipped adapter: the one nearest the cut), the end classes and the lima, refine and mapping fates of both S-reads, and the read orientation were tabulated (`study/analyses/false_cut_anatomy.py`). A molecule counts as written cleanly when one S-read covers at least 80% of it and at least 20% of no other molecule; the slot budget (`study/analyses/molecule_budget.py`) splits the eight slots of each HiFi read into empty slots, artifact molecules, usable molecules (proper, not fused) whose clean S-read fails lima, refine or mapping, usable molecules skera does not write cleanly, and molecules kept.

## Fate

lima 2.13.0 was run on skera S-reads in Iso-Seq mode and in plain mode to obtain primer pairs; isoseq refine 4.3.0 as run and with polyA required at 5, 8, 12 and 20 nt; membership in the existing mapped fltnc BAM was taken from the BAM by ZMW and S-read index. To test the molecules skera does not write, every proper unfused molecule of 3,000 mirror and 3,000 complete reads of the calibration set was cut at our coordinates, written with the read group of a skera S-read BAM, and run through lima --isoseq --peek-guess and isoseq refine with the existing primers, then mapped with minimap2 2.30 (`-ax splice:hq -uf`) to GRCh38; pbmm2 and its index were not available (`study/analyses/downstream_test.py`). In reads with a single mirror junction holding a TSO-TSO molecule, each proper molecule was matched to skera's S-reads by their parent-read coordinates, and skera 1.2.0's list of found adapters (`found_adapters.csv.gz`) to our adapters within 10 bp (`study/analyses/mirror_arms.py`).

## Concatenation bias

For complete arrays, the mean length of the seven partners of each index molecule was compared, by index length, with two nulls of 200 replicates each: null P permutes lengths between arrays within each position; M_T draws positions independently from their own length distributions and accepts arrays with probability s(T), fitted by iterative proportional fitting so that the simulated array-length distribution matches the observed one. The adjacency statistic D is the rank correlation of lengths at distance 1 minus the mean at distances 2 to 7, with 200 bootstrap resamples of arrays; the residual D is D minus its mean under M_T, with an interval from the bootstrap and M_T spreads combined. HiFi yield w(T) was estimated as the HiFi share among annotated HiFi and fail reads of each length, each read weighted by the inverse of its sampling probability, and from polymerase read length and the rate of rq of at least 0.99 per pass count; arrays were reweighted by 1/w(T), clipped at the 99th percentile. The reweighted partner means were compared with independent positions in the reweighted population: a partner at position j has the weighted mean of position j, whatever the index (`ipw_independence_null` in `study/pipeline/stats.py`), with 200 bootstrap resamples of arrays and the weights held fixed.

## ArgenTag

The public 8-fold S-read BAM ({arg_sreads|fmt} S-reads, skera 1.2.0) was downloaded from PacBio's public dataset server; its HiFi read count ({arg_hifi_reads|fmt}) is taken from Table 1 of the ArgenTag and PacBio technical note. Every HiFi ZMW of the cell has S-reads or a record in skera's `non_passing.bam` (availability: `docs/data.md`). Those {arg_non_passing_reads|fmt} parent reads were annotated with stage 02 of this pipeline at HiFi thresholds (`study/argentag/annotate_non_passing.py`) and summarised with `full_read_view.py`; `independent_scan.py` re-called mirror reads in {arg_independent_sample|fmt} random reads with its own minimal adapter search. For the S-read flag, per ZMW we used only the documented S-read tags: the adapter span of its S-reads from `dl` and `dr`, forward order (`dl` below `dr` for every S-read), the start of its first S-read in the parent read (lowest `qs`) and the mean written molecule (highest `qe` minus lowest `qs`, over the number of S-reads). The flag threshold was set on the three BD144 libraries against the full-read calls: at 1, 1.5 and 2 mean written molecules, non-mirror reads were flagged in up to {non_mirror_flagged_1_0_max|pct2}, {non_mirror_flagged_1_5_max|pct2} and {bd144_flag_non_mirror_max|pct2} of HiFi reads, while ND sensitivity was {nd_sensitivity_1_0|f2}, {nd_sensitivity_1_5|f2} and {nd_flag_sensitivity|f2}. For the version check, skera 1.2.0 (bioconda pbskera 1.2.0) was run on the calibration set and both tests were computed for both versions (`head_version_check.py`). The TSO-at-both-ends check takes the first {tso_both_ends_checked|fmt} skera 1.2.0 S-reads of the calibration set and looks for the TSO core within 80 bp of each end (up to 3 edits, either strand; `tso_end_validation.py`).

## Limitations

One library per arm. The arms are technical replicates (beads split into separate cell pools after lysis, separate RT-PCR reactions from the same mix), so a difference between arms is depletion plus replicate variation; the TSO-TSO rate before depletion is only observable in ND, and HM and kin are assumed to have started from a similar rate. Arrays lost before sequencing (for example very short mirror arrays removed by size selection) are not observable, so the cost of skipping depletion may be larger than measured. The ArgenTag comparison rests on one cell-line library; its mirror share counts the reads skera did not pass, so mirror reads that skera passed are missing (a small share in ND). Which arm skera writes is an observation on our reads with skera 26.1.0 and 1.2.0, and where skera cuts at a false cut an observation with skera 26.1.0; neither is documented behaviour. Intervals for the HiFi-yield correction hold w(T) fixed, and the residual D interval leaves out the uncertainty of the M_T fit.

## Software

Python {version_python|str}, polars {version_polars|str}, pysam {version_pysam|str}, edlib {version_edlib|str}, numpy {version_numpy|str}, scipy {version_scipy|str}, plotly {version_plotly|str}, kaleido {version_kaleido|str}; lima 2.13.0, isoseq refine 4.3.0, minimap2 2.30, samtools 1.13 with htslib 1.13 (calibration set); skera 26.1.0 (BD144 S-reads, as delivered) and skera 1.2.0 (ArgenTag S-reads, as published; version check).
